--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::lock` has no `unlock`, because the lock's lifetime is the handle's — `close` frees it, and a closed handle can no longer ask for one — spec § 14, `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\IO\File::lock, Core\IO\File::close
// `flock`'s third mode, `LOCK_UN`, is not a member of this class. The lock
// belongs to the descriptor, so it goes when the descriptor goes: `close`, or
// the end of the request for a handle a program never closes, since the
// request is what owns the table the descriptor sits in.
Core\IO\File $held = Core\IO::open("turns.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
$held->lock();

Core\IO\File $next = Core\IO::open("turns.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
try {
    $next->lock();
    echo "took a lock that was held", "\n";
} catch (IOError $busy) {
    echo "refused while held", "\n";
}

// The same call, after the holder closes. Nothing had to be told the lock was
// free and nothing was waiting to be woken — the second handle simply asks
// again and this time the answer is yes.
$held->close();
$next->lock();
echo "taken after close", "\n";
$next->close();

// A closed handle is a `RuntimeError` and not an `IOError`, for every other
// member's reason: the file is not busy, the program is asking something of a
// handle it has already given up.
try {
    $next->lock();
    echo "locked a closed handle", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $closed) {
    echo "closed: lock", "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
refused while held
taken after close
closed: lock
